    <statement><p>An angle <m>\alpha</m> in standard position has a terminal side illustrated below.  Click on the picture for a larger version.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/sec-trig-intro-8-4-image-1.png" width="50%"/>
<p>Using the grids provided, determine sufficiently accurate values for a point (x,y) and corresponding radius r.  Using these values, determine reasonable approximations for the following:</p>
<p><m>\sin(\alpha)</m>  = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/>.</p>
<p><m>\cos(\alpha)</m>  = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/>.</p>
<p><m>\tan(\alpha)</m>  = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0003"/>.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-0.992278</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-0.124035</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>8</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Pick any point, except the origin, on the terminal side of the angle.  I'll pick <m>(-1,-8)</m>. Use the Pythagorean Theorem to find <m>r</m>.</p>
<p>The distance from <m>(-1,-8)</m> to the origin is 
<me>r=\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-8)^2} = \sqrt{65} \approx 8.06226</me>  
Plug into the definition:</p>
<p><m>\sin(\alpha) = -8/8.06226 = -0.992278</m></p>
<p><m>\cos(\alpha) = -1/8.06226 = -0.124035</m></p>
<p><m>\tan(\alpha) = (-8)/(-1) = 8</m></p>
</solution>
